Hydra is a brute forcing tool to speed up password cracking

Supports dozens of protocols

Utilizes 16 cores by default, AKA it tries 16 passwords simultaneously

brute force FTP with username: user and password list: passlist.txt:
hydra -l user -P passlist.txt ftp://<targetIP>

Use Hydra on a web form:
hydra -l <username> -P <full path to password list> <target IP> -t 4 ssh

-l specifies UN for SSH login
-P list of password
-t specifies the number of threads to use (4 in this case)

Post Web Form:
* Must know which type of request it's using (use devtools)
sudo hydra <UN> <wordlist> <Target IP> http-<Type of request>-form "<path>:<login_credentials>:<invalid response>" -V

<login_credentials> is the name of the input field for the username and password, separated by a colon. For example, if the login form has input fields named "username" and "password", you would use "username=^USER^&password=^PASS^".

-V for verbose output of every attempt

-s before the -V specifies the port if it's not the default (e.g., -s 8080 for port 8080)